# /commit — Smart Commit

**Usage**: `/commit`

## Current State

- Staged changes: !`git diff --cached --stat`
- Unstaged changes: !`git diff --stat`
- Untracked files: !`git status --short | head -10`
- Recent commits: !`git log --oneline -5`

---

## What This Command Does

Reviews all staged and unstaged changes, drafts a conventional commit message, and creates the commit. Follows the [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) specification.

---

## Execution Instructions

---

### Step 1 — Review all changes

Examine the current git state:

```bash
git status
git diff --cached          # staged changes (will be committed)
git diff                   # unstaged changes (will NOT be committed unless staged)
```

If there are no staged changes and no unstaged changes:
```
Nothing to commit — working tree clean.
```
Stop here.

If there are unstaged changes but nothing staged, suggest what to stage:
```
No staged changes. Unstaged changes detected in:
  {file list}

Would you like me to stage these changes? [Y/n]
```

---

### Step 2 — Analyze the changes

Read the actual diff content to understand what changed and why:

```bash
git diff --cached          # for staged changes
git diff                   # for unstaged changes (if staging was approved)
```

Classify the changes:

| Type | When to use |
|------|-------------|
| `feat` | A new feature or capability |
| `fix` | A bug fix |
| `refactor` | Code restructuring with no behavior change |
| `test` | Adding or updating tests |
| `docs` | Documentation changes only |
| `chore` | Build, CI, dependency updates, tooling |
| `style` | Formatting, whitespace, linting (no logic change) |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |

If changes span multiple types, use the most significant one for the commit message.

---

### Step 3 — Pre-commit verification

Before committing, run relevant checks to ensure the code is in good shape:
{{ range .Stacks }}
{{- if eq .Language "go" }}

**{{ .Name }} (Go)**:
```bash
go build ./...
go vet ./...
go test ./... 2>&1 | tail -5
```
{{- end }}
{{- if eq .Language "nodejs" }}

**{{ .Name }} (Node.js)**:
```bash
npm run lint 2>&1 | tail -5    # if lint script exists
npm test 2>&1 | tail -5        # if test script exists
```
{{- end }}
{{- if eq .Language "python" }}

**{{ .Name }} (Python)**:
```bash
pytest -q 2>&1 | tail -5      # if tests exist
```
{{- end }}
{{ end }}

If any check fails, warn the user:
```
⚠  Pre-commit check failed:
    {command}: {failure summary}

Commit anyway? [y/N]
```

Default is NO — do not commit if checks fail unless the user explicitly approves.

---

### Step 4 — Draft the commit message

Format: `{type}({scope}): {short description}`

Rules:
- **type**: one of feat, fix, refactor, test, docs, chore, style, perf
- **scope**: optional — the component, module, or area changed (e.g., `api`, `auth`, `config`)
- **short description**: imperative mood, lowercase, no period, under 72 characters
- **body** (optional): add if the "why" is not obvious from the description alone

Examples:
```
feat(auth): add JWT refresh token rotation
fix(api): handle null response from external provider
refactor(config): extract validation into separate module
test(worker): add integration tests for retry logic
chore: update dependencies to latest patch versions
```

Present the proposed message to the user:
```
Proposed commit message:

  {type}({scope}): {description}

  {body, if applicable}

Proceed? [Y/n]
```

---

### Step 5 — Create the commit

```bash
git add {files}    # if user approved staging unstaged changes
git commit -m "{type}({scope}): {description}"
```

If a body is needed, use a heredoc:
```bash
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
{type}({scope}): {description}

{body}
EOF
)"
```

---

### Step 6 — Confirm

Print the result:
```
Committed: {short hash} {type}({scope}): {description}
  {N} files changed, {N} insertions(+), {N} deletions(-)
```

---

## Error Conditions

| Situation | Action |
|-----------|--------|
| Nothing to commit | "Working tree clean — nothing to commit." Stop. |
| Pre-commit hook fails | Show the hook output. Do NOT retry with --no-verify. Fix the issue first. |
| Merge conflict markers in staged files | "Merge conflict markers found in {file}. Resolve conflicts before committing." Stop. |
| Staged files include secrets (.env, credentials) | "Sensitive file detected: {file}. Remove from staging before committing." Stop. |
